{ What would make a rook trade favorable, and which minor-piece square must you control? Explain Black's plan after any necessary White reply. Answer: Both d-pawns have left the d-file, and the queens are gone. A direct mating race has been replaced by piece coordination and control of d4 and d5. Develop the remaining pieces and contest the d-file. Keep e5 defended, especially if a bishop pins Nf6. A rook on an open file needs support and entry squares. White: White may invade d5 with a knight or double rooks. A passive black bishop can be worse than a small pawn weakness. }
*

